.TH "restoreToTime" "1" "" "SequoiaDB User Manuals" ""
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
restoreToTime \- restore the cluster to a consistent time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]db.restoreToTime([options])\f[]
.SH CATEGORY
.PP
Sdb
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
This function is used to restore the cluster with the Restore mode turned on to
the specified time.
All data changes committed after the time will be rolled back and currently
uncommitted transactions will be terminated.
.SH PARAMETERS
.PP
options ( \f[I]object, required\f[] )
.PP
Set the time point to be restored, the available options are as follows.
.IP \[bu] 2
Time ( \f[I]number/string/Timestamp\f[] ): Specify the target time point of
restoration, in seconds.
.RS 2
.PP
When the value of this parameter is 0, it will be restored to the latest
consistent point in time; when the value is a string, the value filled in should
conform to the ISO 8601 format.
.PP
Format: \f[C]Time: 0\f[] or \f[C]Time: 1609430400\f[] or
\f[C]Time: "2021\-01\-01T00:00:00+08:00"\f[] or
\f[C]Time: Timestamp("2021\-01\-01T00:00:00+08:00")\f[]
.RE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[]
.PP
On Unix\-based machines use \f[C]date\f[] to get or format timestamps.
.IP \[bu] 2
Get the current time in ISO 8601 time.
.RS 2
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ date \-Iseconds 
2021\-01\-01T00:00:00+08:00
\f[]
.fi
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
Format date to ISO 8601 format.
.RS 2
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ date \-Iseconds \-\-date=\[aq]2021/01/01 15:11:09\[aq]
2021\-01\-01T15:11:09+08:00
\f[]
.fi
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
Get the current timestamp.
.RS 2
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ date +%s
1609430400
\f[]
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SH RETURN VALUE
.PP
When the function executes successfully, there is no return value.
.PP
When the function fails, an exception will be thrown and an error message will
be printed.
.SH ERRORS
.PP
The common exceptions of \f[C]restoreToTime()\f[] function are as follows:
.PP
